# auditd rules Sentinel's host-integrity detection depends on.
#
# Installed to /etc/audit/rules.d/sentinel.rules and loaded with augenrules.
# The `sentinel-ingest` collector tails /var/log/audit/audit.log and turns these
# into events; without them, the host.* rules in the detection catalog have
# nothing to fire on.
#
# Deliberately narrow. auditd can be made to log almost everything, and a rule
# set that generates 50k events a day gets disabled by whoever is on call. Every
# watch here corresponds to a specific detection rule.
#
# Keys map to detection rules:
#   sentinel_identity  -> auth.new_user
#   sentinel_ssh       -> auth.new_ssh_key
#   sentinel_cron      -> host.cron_change
#   sentinel_systemd   -> host.systemd_unit_added
#   sentinel_webroot   -> host.webroot_write
#   sentinel_exec      -> host.suspicious_exec
#   sentinel_priv      -> auth.sudo_failures
#   sentinel_cmd       -> the command history (no detection rule; it is
#                         an archive, and archives are read, not alerted on)

# --- Accounts and credentials ----------------------------------------------
# Adding a user or a key is a classic persistence step, and it is rare enough
# on a server like this that every occurrence is worth an alert.
-w /etc/passwd  -p wa -k sentinel_identity
-w /etc/shadow  -p wa -k sentinel_identity
-w /etc/group   -p wa -k sentinel_identity
-w /etc/gshadow -p wa -k sentinel_identity
-w /etc/sudoers   -p wa -k sentinel_priv
-w /etc/sudoers.d -p wa -k sentinel_priv

# --- SSH --------------------------------------------------------------------
# A write to authorized_keys is persistence. A change to sshd_config could be
# anyone widening the way in.
#
# /home is watched whole because the kernel cannot glob: there is no way to say
# "only /home/*/.ssh/". `-w` takes one path, and `-F dir=` takes one subtree;
# neither can express a basename pattern. Generating one watch per existing home
# at install time was considered and rejected: a home created afterwards would
# be silently unwatched, which is the failure mode you never notice.
#
# That makes this the broadest rule in the file — every file any user writes
# anywhere in their home directory arrives here. The narrowing happens
# downstream, in two places:
#
#   collectors/auditd.py  classifies a write whose resolved path has nothing to
#                         do with SSH as a plain `file_write`, so the event is
#                         still stored but no longer claims to be a key change;
#   detect/intrusion.py   filters the same paths again in SQL, for rows already
#                         written under the old label and in case the collector
#                         regresses.
#
# Both use one shared list of path patterns, so they cannot drift apart.
# Without that filter the rule reported ordinary writes in a home directory as
# an SSH key change: 62 critical detections in one deployment, on evidence that
# named `=` and `rotateCount` as files.
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config -p wa -k sentinel_ssh
-w /root/.ssh           -p wa -k sentinel_ssh
-w /home                -p wa -k sentinel_ssh

# --- Scheduled execution ----------------------------------------------------
-w /etc/crontab    -p wa -k sentinel_cron
-w /etc/cron.d     -p wa -k sentinel_cron
-w /etc/cron.daily -p wa -k sentinel_cron
-w /var/spool/cron -p wa -k sentinel_cron
-w /etc/systemd/system -p wa -k sentinel_systemd
-w /usr/lib/systemd/system -p wa -k sentinel_systemd

# --- Webroots ---------------------------------------------------------------
# A file appearing in a webroot outside a deploy window is the classic webshell
# signature. Exclusions for CMS caches and logs are applied downstream, in
# detection.yaml, rather than here — auditd has no useful glob support and
# tuning at the kernel level would mean reloading rules to change a threshold.
-w /var/www -p wa -k sentinel_webroot
-w /srv     -p wa -k sentinel_webroot

# --- Suspicious execution ---------------------------------------------------
# Network tools executed by a non-root user. This is what a reverse shell or a
# staged download looks like from the kernel's point of view.
#
# The auid>=1000 filter excludes system daemons; auid!=unset excludes processes
# with no login session, which would otherwise include every service on the box.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/nc     -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/ncat   -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/socat  -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/wget   -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F path=/usr/bin/curl   -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_exec

# --- SUID changes -----------------------------------------------------------
# A new SUID binary is a privilege-escalation foothold.
#
# The mode-bit filter is the whole rule. Without it this matches EVERY chmod by
# every logged-in user, which on a host where anyone deploys anything means
# hundreds of events per deployment — and, because they shared a key with the
# network tools below, they were reported as "attacker tool executed: install".
# A rule that fires on `chmod 0644` is not watching for privilege escalation,
# it is watching for work being done.
#
# 07000 is setuid|setgid|sticky. Two rules because the mode argument is a1 for
# chmod and fchmod but a2 for fchmodat — one rule covering all three would
# silently test the wrong argument for the third.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S chmod,fchmod -F a1&07000 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_suid
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S fchmodat -F a2&07000 -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_suid

# --- Module loading ---------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel module insertion on a VPS that loads no custom modules is not routine.
# No auid filter here, deliberately: a rootkit loads its module from whatever
# context it can reach, and a module arriving with no login session behind it is
# more alarming than one that has.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S init_module,finit_module,delete_module -k sentinel_module

# --- Command history --------------------------------------------------------
# Every command run inside a login session. This is the one rule in this file
# that is NOT narrow, and the header above explains why that is normally how
# audit rule sets die. It is here anyway, deliberately, and the thing that keeps
# it survivable is the filter, not the volume:
#
#   auid!=unset excludes everything without a login session behind it — every
#   daemon, every cron job, every process in the six Docker containers on this
#   host. Measured on 24 August 2026, that is the difference between a few
#   thousand records a day and tens of thousands an hour.
#
# The consequence, written down because it is a real gap and not an oversight:
# `docker exec -it <container> sh` gives a shell whose commands are NOT recorded,
# because processes inside the container carry no auid. The `docker exec` itself
# IS recorded, with its arguments, so the moment of entry is visible even though
# what follows is not. Closing that gap means auditing every execve on the host,
# which is the thing this file refuses to do.
#
# Both architectures. A 32-bit binary on an x86_64 kernel enters through the b32
# table, and a rule set that watches only b64 has a hole exactly the size of
# "compile it as 32-bit" — one of the oldest audit evasions there is.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_cmd
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F auid!=unset -k sentinel_cmd

# --- Backlog ----------------------------------------------------------------
# How many records the kernel will hold while auditd falls behind. Past this,
# records are DROPPED, and a dropped record looks exactly like a command that
# was never run — the history goes quiet precisely during the busy minute when
# someone is working fast, which is the minute that matters.
#
# The default of 320 was sized for the narrow rule set above it. With execve on
# every login-session command, a single deploy can produce thousands of records
# in a burst. 8192 costs about 8 MB of kernel memory on this host, which has
# 7.6 GB.
#
# The counter is not left to be trusted: `sentinel/selfcheck` reads `lost` from
# `auditctl -s` and alerts when it grows, because a wider buffer makes loss
# rarer, never impossible.
-b 8192

# Wait rather than drop, when the backlog does fill. 60000 is measured in
# 1/100 s of a jiffy — 600 ms — after which the kernel gives up waiting and
# resumes dropping. A syscall that pauses briefly is a machine that feels slow;
# a dropped record is a history that lies.
--backlog_wait_time 60000

# --- What is NOT watched, and why ------------------------------------------
#
# /var/lib/docker: container churn writes there constantly. Watching it would
#   produce thousands of events an hour saying "Docker is running".
#
# /opt/sentinel and /var/lib/sentinel: Sentinel would alert on its own writes,
#   which is both noise and a feedback loop.
#
# Every open()/read(): this is where audit rule sets go to die. The volume is
#   enormous, the signal is negligible, and the result is that someone disables
#   auditd entirely — leaving you with no host integrity monitoring at all.
#
# If another product on this host writes continuously to its own directory, add
# a matching `never,exit` line for it rather than letting it drown the log.
#
# A `-F dir=` rule is only accepted by the kernel if the path EXISTS at load
# time, and `auditctl -R` stops at the first rule it cannot add — so one line
# naming an absent directory silently costs every rule after it. The installer
# therefore leaves such a line out of /etc/audit/rules.d/sentinel.rules on the
# hosts where the directory is missing, and prints which one and why. That is
# also why the directory is not simply created: a /var/lib/docker invented by
# the installer would claim docker is here, and the rule would vanish from the
# kernel again the moment anything removed it (measured, 26 August 2026).
#
# Order matters here for one narrow reason, and it is the reason /var/lib/docker
# is last. The installer can only look at the host at install time. A directory
# that disappears BETWEEN deploys — docker uninstalled, say — makes the kernel
# refuse the rule at the next boot, and `auditctl -R` stops there, taking every
# following rule with it. Sentinel's own two directories exist for as long as
# Sentinel does, so putting them first means a third-party directory that goes
# away costs only itself. These are `never` rules on disjoint subtrees, so their
# relative order changes nothing else.
-a never,exit -F dir=/opt/sentinel
-a never,exit -F dir=/var/lib/sentinel
-a never,exit -F dir=/var/lib/docker

# --- Bait files (canaries) ---------------------------------------------------
# Files with no legitimate reason to ever be read. Nothing on this host opens
# them: no locate/mlocate/plocate index, no antivirus or rootkit scanner
# (clamav/rkhunter/aide), no backup tool (borg/restic/duplicity), no scan timer
# -- measured on the host on 30 August 2026, not assumed. Their content and
# creation live in deploy/install.sh (install_canary_baits); this is only the
# kernel side of it.
#
# Both paths sit outside every watch above on purpose: /root itself is not
# watched (only /root/.ssh is, by sentinel_ssh), so a read here cannot collide
# with an existing rule and arrive under the wrong action.
#
# -p r, not -p rwxa. A `stat()` -- what `ls` and `find` do, including the
# operator's own diagnostics -- is neither a read, a write, an execute nor an
# attribute change; the kernel does not classify it under any of the four
# letters, so even `-p rwxa` would stay silent on a listing. The real
# difference is in the other direction: `a` fires on any bulk attribute sweep
# that never opens the content -- `chmod -R`, `chown -R`, `restorecon` after an
# SELinux relabel, a backup tool restoring permissions -- and `w` fires on that
# same tool restoring the file's bytes. None of those exist on this host today,
# but the design assumes one might tomorrow, and reading the CONTENT is the
# one thing a bait exists to catch. `-p r` is the only choice that stays quiet
# through all of the above and still fires on the one action that matters.
# What it misses: an attacker who only stats or chmods the file without ever
# opening it gains nothing from that, so the miss costs nothing a bait was
# meant to catch.
#
# LAST in the file, deliberately, and for a reason that only exists because of
# a defect these two lines almost caused. `-w` on a path that does not exist
# is REFUSED by the kernel exactly like `-F dir=` on one -- MEASURED on the
# AlmaLinux 9.8 production host (5.14 kernel, auditctl 3.1.5, 30 August 2026),
# after this file shipped with the opposite belief, sourced from an Ubuntu VM
# where it happens not to be true. `deploy/install.sh` now filters an absent
# `-w` path out before offering it to the kernel (see
# `audit_rules_for_this_host`), so a missing bait no longer takes anything down
# with it -- but that fix lives in a second file, and a defence that costs
# nothing belongs here too. Putting these two lines last means that if the
# filter is ever wrong, or a future rule is added the same way without reading
# this comment, the only thing a missing bait can cost is itself. Moved here
# from between "Module loading" and "Command history" on 30 August 2026, for
# exactly that reason.
-w /root/.pgpass          -p r -k sentinel_bait
-w /root/.aws/credentials -p r -k sentinel_bait
